course: dictionary
lesson: 986
title: Boatload and big amounts
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A boatload is the amount a boat can carry, or the amount it is carrying. Read the clues around the word. «Six boatloads of Rats would land in the garden» tells you that six boats, each full of rats, arrive together. The word «land» is a clue that boats are involved.
explain: The word also has an informal meaning: a very large number or amount. «a boatload of homework» does not mean homework on a boat. It means a huge pile of homework. Test it by swapping in «a lot of»: «a lot of homework» still makes sense, so you have found the meaning.
explain: Boatloads can help with maths. If 60 people go in boats that hold 12 each, you need 5 boatloads. If 61 people go, you still need 6 boatloads, because a part-full boat still has to make a trip. So you round up when there is a remainder.
fact: A boatload is the amount a boat can carry or is carrying.
fact: Informally, «a boatload of» means a very large number or amount, such as «a boatload of homework».
fact: Two common phrases are «a boatload of» and «by the boatload».
fact: «Six boatloads of Rats» in the story means six boats, each full of rats.
fact: In maths problems about boatloads, round up if there is a remainder, because a part-full boat still has to make a trip.
exercise: «Six boatloads of Rats would land in the garden.» What does boatloads mean here? | Six small rats | *Six boats, each full of rats | Six gardens | Six rats running fast
exercise: Mum says, «We have a boatload of washing up.» What does boatload mean? | A tiny drop | *A very large amount | A boat that is broken | A bath full of soap
exercise: «Each boatload holds 12 people. How many boatloads for 61 people?» | 12 | 7 | 5 | *6
test: «Six boatloads of Rats, with pistols and cutlasses, will come up the river.» A cutlass is what kind of thing? | *A short curved sword | A kind of boat | A large river fish | A type of rat
test: Jess says «a boatload of homework» is about a boat. What is the answer? | Yes, she must do it on a boat | *No, it means a very large amount of homework | Yes, boatload means a small boat | No, it means a tiny amount
exam: A school trip has 50 children, and each boat holds 8 children. How many boatloads are needed? | 50 | 8 | 6 | *7
